{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 16, :comments ["And the reason for <b>I also will do this unto you</b> — by way of truth, it is like the inversion: אף אני זאת אעשה לכם. And this is why He constantly mentions in this pericope \"I\": \"I also will walk contrary unto you\" (Leviticus 26:24), \"I also will smite you\" (ibid.), \"I also will chastise you\" (Leviticus 26:28), \"I will bring the land into desolation\" (Leviticus 26:32), \"I also will walk unto them in hostility\" (Leviticus 26:41). And in the midrash of Rabbi Nechunya ben HaKanah: it is not enough that I master the judgment, but I also will chastise, etc. And the general principle is that the Holy One, blessed be He, cuts this covenant, and this is the reason for \"sevenfold for your sins,\" \"sevenfold as your sins\" — and the curses are curses of the covenant, for the utterance is from the mouth of the Gevurah (the Almighty) and in His own tongue: \"I will do,\" \"I have chastised,\" \"I will smite.\" Therefore He said, \"which HaShem set between Him and the children of Israel\" (Leviticus 26:46), for it was in His great Name that He established this covenant. But in Mishneh Torah (Deuteronomy 28:15) He said, \"If you will not hearken to the voice of YHWH your Elohim to observe to do all His commandments,\" and there He constantly uses the language of cursing, and opens with them \"Cursed are you\" (ibid. v. 16), for the blessing is withheld from them, and He said, \"YHWH shall give\" (ibid. v. 24). And this is the statement of our Sages (Megillah 31b): the curses in Torat Kohanim are in the plural, and Moses pronounced them from the mouth of the Gevurah; while those in Mishneh Torah are in the singular, and Moses pronounced them from his own mouth — for the Gevurah made Moses an emissary between Herself and all Israel.\n\nKnow and understand that these curses allude to the first exile, for in the First Temple all the matters of this covenant came to pass — the exile and the redemption from it. Thus you will see in the rebukes that He said (Leviticus 26:15), \"And if you reject My statutes and if your soul abhor My judgments,\" and He said, \"to break My covenant\" (ibid.), and He mentioned among them bamot and sun-images and idols (Leviticus 26:30), for they were worshippers of idolatry and did all the evils. And that is what He said: \"And I will make your sanctuaries desolate, and I will not smell the savour of your sweet incense\" (Leviticus 26:31) — He adds thereto that He will remove from them His sanctuary and the acceptance of the offerings that were a pleasing savour to Him in that Temple. And the punishments upon them — sword and evil beast and pestilence and famine and exile in the end — all of this came to pass there, as is explicitly stated in the book of Jeremiah (32:24). And He said concerning the exile, \"Then shall the land enjoy her sabbaths, etc. all the days of its desolation shall it rest, that which it did not rest\" (Leviticus 26:34–35), for the years of the exile corresponded to the years during which they had annulled the sabbatical years. And thus Scripture said concerning that exile, \"to fulfil the word of YHWH by the mouth of Jeremiah, until the land had enjoyed her sabbaths; all the days that it lay desolate it kept sabbath, to fulfil threescore and ten years\" (II Chronicles 36:21). Thus He warned them, and thus it came upon them. If so, it is clear that Scripture spoke of that exile.\n\nNow observe further the matter of the redemption from it, for He promises only that He will remember the covenant of the fathers and the remembrance of the land (Leviticus 26:42) — not that He will pardon their iniquity and forgive their sin and restore His love for them as before, and not that He will gather their dispersed ones. For so it was when they came up from Babylon: only Judah and Benjamin returned, and the few who joined them from a handful of the tribes that had been exiled to Babylon, and they returned in poverty, in servitude to the kings of Persia. Nor did He say that they would return in complete repentance before Him, but only that they would confess their iniquity and the iniquity of their fathers (Leviticus 26:40). And we find that the men of the Second Temple did so, as Daniel confessed: \"We have sinned and have dealt corruptly and have done wickedly and have rebelled, and have turned aside from Your commandments, etc. to our kings, to our princes, and to our fathers\" (Daniel 9:5–8), and it is written, \"For because of our sins and the iniquities of our fathers, Jerusalem and Your people are become a reproach\" (ibid. v. 16). And so Nehemiah confessed (Nehemiah 1:5–11), and Ezra said, \"Our kings, our princes, our priests, and our fathers have not kept Your Torah\" (ibid. 9:34). Behold, all of them learned from the Torah that they should confess their iniquity and the iniquity of their fathers. And all these matters are explicit in this covenant, that it truly alludes to the first exile and the redemption from it.\n\nBut the covenant in Mishneh Torah alludes to this exile of ours and to the redemption from it, for we have observed at the outset that no end or limit is alluded to there, and in the redemption He promised nothing but made it contingent upon repentance. And He did not mention in those transgressions that they would make Asherim and sun-images and worship idolatry at all, but said, \"And it shall come to pass, if you will not hearken to the voice of YHWH your Elohim, to observe to do all His commandments and His statutes\" (Deuteronomy 28:15) — He said that because they would transgress some of His commandments, not observing and doing all of them, they would be punished. For so it was in the Second Temple, as they said (Yoma 9b): Why was the First Temple destroyed? Because of idolatry, forbidden sexual relations, and bloodshed. The Second Temple, in which we know that they were engaged in Torah and acts of loving-kindness — why was it destroyed? Because of baseless hatred that was among them. And He did not mention there the Temple and the sweet savour as He mentioned here, for the fire did not descend and consume the offerings in the Second Temple, as they testified in tractate Yoma there (21b). And He said in the curses, \"YHWH will bring upon you a nation from far off, from the end of the earth, as the eagle flies\" (Deuteronomy 28:49) — this is the Roman people who came upon them, who were very distant from them. And He said there, \"unto a nation that you have not known\" (ibid. v. 36), \"a nation whose tongue you shall not understand\" (ibid. v. 49), because of the great distance from our land, and not so in the matters of this covenant, for they were exiled to Babylon and Assyria, which are close to the land and do battle with them constantly, and Israel had kinship from there, and they knew their language, as it says, \"Speak, please, to your servants in Aramaic, for we understand it\" (II Kings 18:26). And likewise, \"And YHWH will scatter you among all the peoples, from the one end of the earth even unto the other end of the earth\" (Deuteronomy 28:64) — that is our exile today, for we are scattered from one end of the world to the other. And He said, \"And YHWH will bring you back into Egypt in ships\" (ibid. v. 68), and in this our exile it was that Titus filled ships with them, as is written in the book of the Romans.\n\nAnd likewise what He says there, \"Your sons and your daughters shall be given unto another people, and your eyes shall look on\" (Deuteronomy 28:32), \"Your sons and your daughters shall you beget, but they shall not be yours, for they shall go into captivity\" (ibid. v. 41) — this is not an exile where fathers and sons were exiled together; rather, that captivity was of the children alone while the fathers remained in the land. And this was not said in the first covenant, because there they went into a complete exile. But in the second covenant He mentioned it, for the Romans ruled in our land and took the sons and daughters as they pleased. And likewise, \"And you shall serve your enemies whom YHWH shall send against you, in hunger and in thirst\" (ibid. v. 48) — that is the servitude that we served the Romans in our land, and their officers ruled over the land and laid a heavy yoke upon us, taking our persons and our property, as is well known in the books.\n\nAnd there is a further proof, for He said, \"YHWH will lead you and your king whom you have set over you, unto a nation that neither you nor your fathers have known\" (ibid. v. 36) — for King Agrippa went to Rome at the end of the Second Temple period, and on account of his going there the Temple was destroyed. And Scripture did not say \"the king who shall reign over you,\" but said \"your king whom you have set\" — He hinted to us, blessed be He, that he was not fit to reign, and it was forbidden for him to be king over Israel by the law of the Torah, but they set him and his fathers over themselves unlawfully, as this is mentioned in tractate Sotah (Sotah 41a).\n\nAnd all these hints such as these explicitly set forth the matter of this exile of ours. And the redemption in that second covenant is a complete redemption, superior to all others. He said, \"And it shall come to pass, when all these things are come upon you, the blessing and the curse,\" etc. (Deuteronomy 30:1), and He promised, \"and He will do you good, and multiply you above your fathers\" (ibid. v. 5) — which is a promise to all the tribes of Israel, not to a sixth of the people. And there He promised that He would cut off and destroy those who exiled us, as it says, \"And YHWH your Elohim will put all these curses upon your enemies, and on them that hate you, that persecuted you\" (ibid. v. 7). And behold, \"your enemies\" and \"those that hate you\" are a hint to the two nations that will constantly pursue us. And these matters promise in the future redemption a more complete promise than all the visions of Daniel.\n\nAnd likewise what He said here, \"And your enemies shall be astonished at it\" (Leviticus 26:32) — this is good tidings proclaimed in all the exiles, that our land does not receive our enemies. And this too is a great proof and a promise to us, for you will not find in all the inhabited world a land that is good and broad and was ever settled — and is as desolate as it is. For since we left it, no nation or tongue has taken it, and all strive to settle it but to no avail.\n\nAnd behold, the first covenant that is in this pericope the Holy One, blessed be He, established, for so was His great Name with us in the First Temple. And the second covenant, which is in the pericote of Vehayah Ki Tavo, was spoken by Moses, hinting at the complete withdrawal of His Shekhinah, for in the Second Temple there was only the honor of His Name, as it says, \"I will take pleasure in it, and I will be glorified\" (Haggai 1:8). And the addition of the letter ה (heh) is a hint to the second ה (heh) in the Great Name. And they already expounded it (Yoma 21b) according to another midrash as well — a hint to the five things of which the Second Temple was deprived, etc."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 26, :register {:flags {0 ["the Almighty"]}}, :notes {0 "The opening phrase 'on the way of truth' (על דרך האמת) introduces a kabbalistic reading concerning the inversion of letters in the verse; the hint is left cryptic per the translator's convention. The reading of Haggai 1:8 follows Ramban's text, which differs slightly from the standard Masoretic vocalization."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:01:46.090164699Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}